{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-07-repeated-replacement",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Repeated Replacement",
    "description": "Use a retention factor to track how much original liquid survives repeated equal draw-and-refill operations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Derive the retention factor from a single draw-and-refill, then multiply that factor across repeated operations. Practise pure liquid left, replacement liquid present, final ratios, reverse-capacity problems, and the limits of the shortcut. Keep the exponent tied to the number of complete operations.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 29,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Build the repeated-replacement formula from one operation: if a vessel of x units loses y units of a well-mixed liquid, the fraction of the original pure liquid retained is (1 − y/x). After n identical operations, pure liquid left is x(1 − y/x)^n. Cover finding pure liquid left, replacement liquid present, final ratio, and reversing the formula to recover the original capacity when the final ratio is known. Include three and four operation patterns like the chapter's milk and wine examples. Emphasise that the exponent counts complete remove-and-refill rounds and that the shortcut applies only when the vessel returns to the same total volume after each round."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's track what survives repeated replacement.\n\nA vessel starts with pure milk. Each round removes some mixture and refills with water. After the first round, later samples are no longer pure milk — so the milk lost each time becomes smaller.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A 40 L vessel of pure milk loses 4 L, then gets 4 L water.\n\nThe fraction of milk retained after one operation is:\n1−4/40 = 9/10.\n\nMilk left = 40×9/10 = 36 L.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The refill restores the total volume, but it does not restore the lost milk.\n\nSo each complete round multiplies the milk amount by the same retention factor, provided the same volume is removed each time.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
